  "text": "umably, I believe I have misinterpreted the problem. Let's reanalyze the problem and correct any mistakes.\n\nDefine \\( n!! \\) to be:\n\\[ n!! = \\begin{cases} \nn(n-3)(n-6) \\cdots 3 \\cdot 0 & \\text{if } n \\text{ is a multiple of } 3 \\\\\nn(n-3)(n-6) \\cdots 4 \\cdot 1 & \\text{if } n \\text{ is one more than a multiple of } 3 \n\\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe are asked to find the sum:\n\\[ \\sum_{i=1}^{2010} \\frac{(3i-2)!!}{(3i)!!} \\]\n\nFirst, we need to understand the terms \\( (3i-2)!! \\) and \\( (3i)!! \\):\n- For \\( (3i-2)!! \\), it is the product of every other integer starting from \\( 3i-2 \\) down to 0, which is an empty product (1) since it starts at an odd number.\n- For \\( (3i)!! \\), it is the product of every third integer starting from 3i down to 0.\n\nThus, the sum simplifies to:\n\\[ \\sum_{i=1}^{2010} \\frac{1}{(3i)!!} \\]\n\nSince \\( (3i)!! \\) is an empty product for odd \\( i \\), the sum is zero for odd \\( i \\). For even \\( i \\), \\( (3i)!! \\) is the product of every third integer starting from \\( 3i \\) down to 0, which is a non-zero product.\n\nHowever, the problem asks for the sum in terms of \\( \\frac{a}{b} \\) and \\( \\frac{c}{3^a} \\). Since the sum is zero for odd \\( i \\), we only need to consider even \\( i \\).\n\nFor even \\( i \\), \\( (3i)!! \\) is a non-zero product. The denominator of each term \\( \\frac{1}{(3i)!!} \\) will be a product of powers of 3 and other integers.\n\nTo find the denominator in lowest terms, we need to consider the least common multiple (LCM) of the denominators. The LCM of the denominators will be a product of the highest powers of 3 and other integers that appear in the denominators.\n\nThe LCM of the denominators will be \\( 3^a \\cdot c \\), where \\( c \\) is not a multiple of 3.\n\nThus, the denominator is \\( 3^a \\cdot c \\), and we need to find \\( \\frac{ac}{15} \\).\n\nSince \\( 15 = 3 \\cdot 5 \\), we need \\( \\frac{ac}{15} \\) to be an integer. This means \\( c \\) must be a multiple of 5.\n\nLet's assume \\( c = 5k \\) for some integer \\( k \\). Then \\( \\frac{ac}{15} = \\frac{a \\cdot 5k}{15} = \\frac{ak}{3} \\).\n\nSince \\( \\frac{ak}{3} \\) is an integer, \\( k \\) must be a multiple of 3. Let \\( k = 3m \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Then \\( c = 5 \\cdot 3m = 15m \\).\n\nThus, \\( \\frac{ac}{15} = \\frac{a \\cdot 15m}{15} = am \\).\n\nSince \\( a \\) and \\( m \\) are integers, \\( \\frac{ac}{15} \\) is an integer.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]",
